 ],
 "literal": "Bangles slipped indeed, shoulders thinned indeed, / is there releasing of modesty, O friend? In the mountain cleft's cave, / along with the mountain side, spreading fragrance, swaying clusters of glory lily's / fragrant pollen humming small-winged bee, / like the gem emitted by a snake, appears / on the mountain fenced with mature bamboo, to its lord!",
 "translation": "My bangles have slipped off, my shoulders have grown thin. O friend, is there any way to let go of my modesty? On the mountain of the lord, fenced with mature bamboo, a small-winged bee hums over the fragrant pollen of the swaying clusters of glory lilies that spread their scent all along the mountain clefts and caves, looking like a glowing gem spat out by a snake.",
 "modern": "தோழி! என் வளையல்கள் கழன்றுவிட்டன; தோள்களும் மெலிந்துவிட்டன. நாணத்தை விட்டுவிடுவதைத் தவிர வேறு வழி உண்டோ? முதிர்ந்த மூங்கில்களை வேலியாகக் கொண்ட மலைக்கு உரிய தலைவனுடைய மலையில், மலைப்பிளவுகளிலும் குகைகளிலும் நறுமணம் கமழுமாறு அசையும் குலைகளையுடைய காந்தள் மலரின் நறிய மகரந்தத்தை ஊதும் சிறிய சிறகுகளையுடைய தும்பி, பாம்பு உமிழ்ந்த மாணிக்கத்தைப் போலத் தோன்றும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend in distress because of her separation from the hero. Her physical suffering is so intense that her shoulders have thinned, causing her bangles to slip off. She asks her friend if she must cast aside her modesty to express her grief openly. She describes the hero's mountain, where a small-winged bee feeding on the pollen of the swaying glory lily looks like a brilliant gem spat out by a snake. This vivid image of the bee inside the red glory lily serves as an implicit metaphor; the snake's gem is precious but dangerous, just as the hero's love is precious but his absence brings deadly pain.",
